          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Orthogonal spectra are one version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/highly+structured+spectra"} highly structured spectra
            that support a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+smash+product+of+spectra"} symmetric monoidal smash product of spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{X_n\}_{n \in \mathbb{N}}
            equipped with maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n \wedge S^1 \longrightarrow X_{n+1}
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension"} suspension
            of one into the next, but such that the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is equipped with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
            and such that the induced structure maps.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∧
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi k
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_n \wedge S^k \longrightarrow X_{n+k}
            are all
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ×
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)\times O(k)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance"} equivariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of such orthogonal spectra and it is equivalent to the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} stable homotopy theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#MMSS08"}> MMSS 98
            ).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %em orthogonal spectra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentation
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} of spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product+of+spectra"} smash product of spectra
            such as to yield itself a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+model+category"} monoidal
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category+of+spectra"} model category of spectra
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+orthogonal+spectra"} model structure on orthogonal spectra
          %p
            This implies in particular that with respect to this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+smash+product+of+spectra"} symmetric smash product of spectra
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            is presented simply as a plain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} in
            orthogonal spectra (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/highly+structured+ring+spectrum"}> highly structured ring spectrum
            ”). See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+ring+spectrum"} orthogonal ring spectrum
          %p
            Other presentations sharing this property are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+spectra"} symmetric spectra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-modules"} S-modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %p
            One advantages of orthogonal spectra over symmetric spectra is that for them the naive definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            comes out homotopically correct, while for symmetric spectra an intransparent replacement is needed first (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "symmetric+spectrum#HomotopyGroups"} symmetric spectrum – Homotopy groups
          %p
            Another advantage is that orthogonal spectra support a similarly good model for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
            with equivariance by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+groups"} compact Lie groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+spectra"} symmetric spectra
            share this property only for equivariance under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+groups"} finite groups
